    "description": "Iran’s provincial administration connects territorial units with the central government while operating alongside councils and sector-specific institutions. Understanding it requires separating administrative hierarchy, local representation, and development planning. [s1][s2][s3]",
    "tags": ["Provincial administration", "Territorial governance", "Local councils", "Development planning"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T23:45:12Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T23:45:12Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Provincial Administration in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "An introduction to Iran’s territorial hierarchy, appointed administration, councils, and provincial development planning."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "territorial-framework",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Reading the territorial hierarchy",
        "content": "Several Persian administrative terms recur in accounts of Iran. An ostan is a province, a shahrestan a county, a bakhsh a district, and a dehestan a rural district. The national divisions law places rural districts under districts, districts under counties, counties under provinces, and provinces under the central administration. Its definition of a province brings together neighboring counties with regard to political, social, cultural, economic, and natural conditions. [s1]\n\nThis hierarchy describes territorial administration. It does not mean that a province is simply its capital city, or that a rural district is a single village. The law identifies villages, rural districts, cities, districts, counties, and provinces separately. Those distinctions help readers interpret administrative addresses and understand why a municipality and a county governorate can cover different territories. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "territorial-diagram",
        "type": "markdown-mermaid",
        "title": "Territorial subordination in the national divisions law",
        "content": "```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n  A[\"Central administration\"] -->|\"Administrative subordination\"| B[\"Province: ostan\"]\n  B -->|\"Administrative subordination\"| C[\"County: shahrestan\"]\n  C -->|\"Administrative subordination\"| D[\"District: bakhsh\"]\n  D -->|\"Administrative subordination\"| E[\"Rural district: dehestan\"]\n```",
        "summary": "The arrows show the territorial hierarchy specified in Article 12. They do not depict elections, council representation, or every public agency’s reporting arrangements.",
        "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "representation-and-administration",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Appointed administration and councils",
        "content": "The governor-general, or ostandar, represents the national government within a province. The administrative resolution assigns responsibilities for implementing public policy and coordinating provincial affairs. County governors, called farmandars, form another part of this appointed administrative structure. [s4]\n\nCouncils belong to a different constitutional framework. Article 100 provides for councils concerned with local welfare and development; Article 101 establishes a Supreme Council of the Provinces composed of provincial council representatives. Article 103 requires government-appointed territorial officials to observe council decisions within the councils’ jurisdiction. These provisions should be read together: national administrative authority and legally bounded local representation coexist. A provincial council is therefore institutionally distinct from the governorate. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "planning",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Development planning has its own institutions",
        "content": "The Provincial Planning and Development Council is another distinct body. Under Article 31 of the permanent development provisions, the governor chairs it and the head of the provincial planning and budget organization serves as secretary. Its composition includes provincial officials, with parliamentary representatives participating as observers. [s3]\n\nThe same article differentiates provincial allocation from county-level decisions. It assigns the provincial council a role in setting county shares of specified development appropriations using population and deprivation indicators, while restricting interference in county planning committee decisions. This is a useful reminder that the word “provincial” does not identify one interchangeable institution: the governorate, representative council, planning body, and sectoral departments have different legal functions. [s3]\n\nThis page establishes that basic institutional map. A complete contemporary account also needs verified evidence about later delegations of authority, operational crisis arrangements, and how coordination works across provinces; those questions remain open in this draft.",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
